The table structure information is (table names): 
['player', 'team', 'match', 'player_match', 'ball_by_ball', 'batsman_scored', 'wicket_taken', 'extra_runs']
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Below are three parts:

1. Key columns & their possible roles  
2. Typical filter / join conditions you may need  
3. Ten exploratory SQLite‐compatible queries (from very simple to the one that actually returns the required TOP‑3 list).  Each query is preceded by a comment that explains what it does.  All queries finish with `LIMIT 20` so you can inspect only a handful of rows while testing.

——————————————————————  
1) Columns that matter (and why)

ball_by_ball.match_id            :  join key to every other “ball” level table  
ball_by_ball.over_id             :  identifies the over (within an innings)  
ball_by_ball.ball_id             :  identifies the specific delivery   
ball_by_ball.innings_no          :  needed to disambiguate overs of different innings in the same match  
ball_by_ball.bowler              :  bowler (player_id) who delivered the ball – we ultimately rank by this  
batsman_scored.runs_scored       :  runs off the bat on that delivery  
extra_runs.extra_runs            :  any extras on that delivery ( wides, no‑balls, byes, … )  
player.player_id / player_name   :  convert bowler id → human readable name  
match.match_id / match_date …    :  to show which match the “big over” occurred in  

——————————————————————  
2) Conditions / join logic you will probably use

•  Join the three ball‑level tables on  
   (match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no).

•  Compute  total_runs_on_ball =
      COALESCE(batsman_scored.runs_scored,0) +
      COALESCE(extra_runs.extra_runs,0).

•  Aggregate total_runs_on_ball by
   (match_id, innings_no, over_id, bowler) to get
   runs_conceded_in_over.

•  For every match take the MAX(runs_conceded_in_over); keep only the rows that equal that max  
   (→ the “worst” over(s) of that match).

•  Across all those “worst overs”, order by runs_conceded_in_over DESC
   and pick the top 3 distinct bowler‑over pairs.

——————————————————————  
3) Ten step‑by‑step queries

```sql
-- 1. See a handful of distinct bowler IDs that appear in ball_by_ball
SELECT DISTINCT bowler
FROM ball_by_ball
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Map some bowler IDs to names to check the linkage with player table
SELECT DISTINCT b.bowler, p.player_name
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
JOIN player        AS p  ON p.player_id = b.bowler
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect bat‑runs and extra‑runs for a single delivery (join of three ball-level tables)
SELECT b.match_id, b.over_id, b.ball_id,
       COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0) AS bat_runs,
       COALESCE(e.extra_runs,0)  AS ext_runs
FROM ball_by_ball  AS b
LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
       ON s.match_id   = b.match_id
      AND s.over_id    = b.over_id
      AND s.ball_id    = b.ball_id
      AND s.innings_no = b.innings_no
LEFT JOIN extra_runs     AS e
       ON e.match_id   = b.match_id
      AND e.over_id    = b.over_id
      AND e.ball_id    = b.ball_id
      AND e.innings_no = b.innings_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Compute total runs given in every over for ONE sample match (335987)
SELECT b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler,
       SUM( COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0) + COALESCE(e.extra_runs,0) ) AS runs_in_over
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
       ON s.match_id   = b.match_id
      AND s.over_id    = b.over_id
      AND s.ball_id    = b.ball_id
      AND s.innings_no = b.innings_no
LEFT JOIN extra_runs     AS e
       ON e.match_id   = b.match_id
      AND e.over_id    = b.over_id
      AND e.ball_id    = b.ball_id
      AND e.innings_no = b.innings_no
WHERE b.match_id = 335987
GROUP BY b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler
ORDER BY runs_in_over DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. For the first 5 matches, find the maximum runs conceded in any over
WITH over_runs AS (
  SELECT b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler,
         SUM( COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0) + COALESCE(e.extra_runs,0) ) AS runs_in_over
  FROM ball_by_ball AS b
  LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
         ON s.match_id=b.match_id AND s.over_id=b.over_id AND s.ball_id=b.ball_id AND s.innings_no=b.innings_no
  LEFT JOIN extra_runs     AS e
         ON e.match_id=b.match_id AND e.over_id=b.over_id AND e.ball_id=b.ball_id AND e.innings_no=b.innings_no
  GROUP BY b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler
)
SELECT match_id, MAX(runs_in_over) AS max_runs_over
FROM over_runs
WHERE match_id IN (SELECT DISTINCT match_id FROM ball_by_ball LIMIT 5)
GROUP BY match_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Show the actual over(s) that equal the max in those five matches
WITH over_runs AS (
  SELECT b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler,
         SUM( COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0) + COALESCE(e.extra_runs,0) ) AS runs_in_over
  FROM ball_by_ball AS b
  LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
         ON s.match_id=b.match_id AND s.over_id=b.over_id AND s.ball_id=b.ball_id AND s.innings_no=b.innings_no
  LEFT JOIN extra_runs     AS e
         ON e.match_id=b.match_id AND e.over_id=b.over_id AND e.ball_id=b.ball_id AND e.innings_no=b.innings_no
  GROUP BY b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler
), max_per_match AS (
  SELECT match_id, MAX(runs_in_over) AS max_runs
  FROM over_runs
  GROUP BY match_id
)
SELECT o.match_id, o.innings_no, o.over_id, o.bowler, o.runs_in_over
FROM over_runs AS o
JOIN max_per_match AS m
  ON m.match_id = o.match_id
 WHERE o.runs_in_over = m.max_runs
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Same logic as #6 but across **all** matches; keep only overs costing >=20 runs
WITH over_runs AS (
  SELECT b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler,
         SUM( COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0) + COALESCE(e.extra_runs,0) ) AS runs_in_over
  FROM ball_by_ball AS b
  LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
         ON s.match_id=b.match_id AND s.over_id=b.over_id AND s.ball_id=b.ball_id AND s.innings_no=b.innings_no
  LEFT JOIN extra_runs     AS e
         ON e.match_id=b.match_id AND e.over_id=b.over_id AND e.ball_id=b.ball_id AND e.innings_no=b.innings_no
  GROUP BY b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler
), worst_over AS (
  SELECT o.*
  FROM over_runs AS o
  JOIN (
     SELECT match_id, MAX(runs_in_over) AS max_runs
     FROM over_runs
     GROUP BY match_id
  ) AS m
  ON m.match_id = o.match_id AND o.runs_in_over = m.max_runs
)
SELECT *
FROM worst_over
WHERE runs_in_over >= 20
ORDER BY runs_in_over DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Rank the “worst overs” by runs and bring the bowler’s name
WITH over_runs AS (
  SELECT b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler,
         SUM( COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0) + COALESCE(e.extra_runs,0) ) AS runs_in_over
  FROM ball_by_ball AS b
  LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
         ON s.match_id=b.match_id AND s.over_id=b.over_id AND s.ball_id=b.ball_id AND s.innings_no=b.innings_no
  LEFT JOIN extra_runs     AS e
         ON e.match_id=b.match_id AND e.over_id=b.over_id AND e.ball_id=b.ball_id AND e.innings_no=b.innings_no
  GROUP BY b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler
), worst_over AS (
  SELECT o.*
  FROM over_runs AS o
  JOIN (
     SELECT match_id, MAX(runs_in_over) AS max_runs
     FROM over_runs
     GROUP BY match_id
  ) AS m
  ON m.match_id = o.match_id AND o.runs_in_over = m.max_runs
)
SELECT w.match_id, w.over_id, w.runs_in_over,
       w.bowler, p.player_name
FROM worst_over AS w
JOIN player      AS p ON p.player_id = w.bowler
ORDER BY w.runs_in_over DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Focus on the absolute top 10 most expensive such overs
WITH over_runs AS (
  SELECT b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler,
         SUM( COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0) + COALESCE(e.extra_runs,0) ) AS runs_in_over
  FROM ball_by_ball AS b
  LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
         ON s.match_id=b.match_id AND s.over_id=b.over_id AND s.ball_id=b.ball_id AND s.innings_no=b.innings_no
  LEFT JOIN extra_runs     AS e
         ON e.match_id=b.match_id AND e.over_id=b.over_id AND e.ball_id=b.ball_id AND e.innings_no=b.innings_no
  GROUP BY b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler
), worst_over AS (
  SELECT o.*
  FROM over_runs AS o
  JOIN (
     SELECT match_id, MAX(runs_in_over) AS max_runs
     FROM over_runs
     GROUP BY match_id
  ) AS m
  ON m.match_id = o.match_id AND o.runs_in_over = m.max_runs
)
SELECT w.match_id, w.innings_no, w.over_id, w.bowler, p.player_name, w.runs_in_over
FROM worst_over AS w
JOIN player        AS p ON p.player_id = w.bowler
ORDER BY w.runs_in_over DESC
LIMIT 10;
```

```sql
-- 10. Finally, get the TOP‑3 bowlers (one row per bowler with his single highest “worst-over”)
WITH over_runs AS (
  SELECT b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler,
         SUM( COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0) + COALESCE(e.extra_runs,0) ) AS runs_in_over
  FROM ball_by_ball AS b
  LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
         ON s.match_id=b.match_id AND s.over_id=b.over_id AND s.ball_id=b.ball_id AND s.innings_no=b.innings_no
  LEFT JOIN extra_runs     AS e
         ON e.match_id=b.match_id AND e.over_id=b.over_id AND e.ball_id=b.ball_id AND e.innings_no=b.innings_no
  GROUP BY b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler
), worst_over AS (
  SELECT o.*
  FROM over_runs AS o
  JOIN (
     SELECT match_id, MAX(runs_in_over) AS max_runs
     FROM over_runs
     GROUP BY match_id
  ) AS m
  ON m.match_id = o.match_id AND o.runs_in_over = m.max_runs
), ranked AS (
  SELECT w.*,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
             PARTITION BY w.bowler
             ORDER BY w.runs_in_over DESC
         ) AS rn
  FROM worst_over AS w
)
SELECT r.match_id,
       r.bowler,
       p.player_name,
       r.runs_in_over
FROM ranked AS r
JOIN player  AS p ON p.player_id = r.bowler
WHERE r.rn = 1        -- keep each bowler's personal best
ORDER BY r.runs_in_over DESC
LIMIT 20;             -- shows top 3 (or up to 20 if many ties)
```

